A1. Bang up good letters for alphabet soup. ~ TNT
A4. You should see this one stalk up after its tips. ~ ASPARAGUS
A12. You'll need this if you're going to swing a cat. ~ ROOM
A14. They called her this because she tolled so much. ~ NELL
A15. Where should prudes, prunes and peruna come from? ~ PERU
A16. A musical tramp. ~ OBOE
A17. This got whipped by mother and licked by dad. ~ CREAM
A19. This was cast for the hero and made a big hit. ~ EGG
A20. Beyond the limit. ~ ULTRA
A22. A good listener. ~ HEARER
A24. Two letters and an abbreviation for each. ~ EA
A25. Appears. ~ SEEMS
A26. The horses are "off" and the race is this. ~ ON
A27. Eventually this will come to dust. ~ MAID
A29. A knock for someone. ~ RAP
A30. A bully rag to wave. ~ RED
A32. This will make you tired? ~ TOIL
A34. That printer's measure again. ~ EM
A36. When a dog bites a flea. ~ NEWS
A38. This is out of ordinary. ~ OR
A40. A popular shade for warm weather. ~ TREE
A42. Why do this? Do you ever listen to a yeast program? ~ AIL
A44. He never could make assent from this. ~ NO
A45. I am contracted. ~ IM
A47. Don't look now but I think they are going to put this on your new white flannels. ~ TAX
A48. One that will always remain single. ~ UNIT
A50. The first Transatlantic Air Co. ~ WE
A52. Twist the ends of this and make a hoop. ~ POOH
A54. Everybody in Washington is in this. ~ DC
A55. Don't try to make light of this. ~ DARK
A57. This is bad in France. ~ MAL
A59. A good place to make a splash. ~ TUB
A62. Something the first telephone did. ~ RANG
A64. Initials of Public Enemy No. 1. ~ PE
A65. A drawback in a fisherman's life. ~ TROLL
A67. This finishes the whoop. ~ LA
A69. Famous feminine mouthpiece. ~ PORTIA
A71. A rootstock by any other name would never smell as sweet. ~ ORRIS
A72. The mode of the upper crust. ~ ALA
A74. Things that Dizzy Dean loves to touch. ~ BASES
A75. This is enough to make anything contrary (prefix). ~ ANTI
A76. This has a large davenport. ~ IOWA
A78. A step in the right direction. ~ PACE
A79. What a man! ~ HERO
A80. This price comes higher than the f.o.b. price. ~ DELIVERED
A81. Poets are glad that this is over. ~ OER

D1. There is many a slip in this. ~ TROUSSEAU
D2. This is kind of rank. ~ NOBLE
D3. One who blows his own horn. ~ TOOTER
D4. A heavyweight with a big drag. (Plur.) ~ ANCHORS
D5. This suggests unclouded tranquility. ~ SERENE
D6. This was made for his suit so that he could keep his shirt. ~ PLEA
D7. This has shattered many a girlhood dream. ~ ALARM
D8. You can make a heap with this. ~ AP
D9. Mr. Whillikins' first name. ~ GEE
D10. Impel. ~ URGE
D11. What they got for raising cane. ~ SUGAR
D13. A sea nymph's boy friend. ~ MERMAN
D18. What the farmers have made from stock this year. ~ MEAT
D21. A tree with the jitters. ~ ASPEN
D23. This turned Pat into a patriot. ~ RIOT
D28. This is how cheap farms are in Kansas. ~ DIRT
D31. This or die. ~ DO
D33. A big help in working crossword puzzles. ~ LEAD
D35. If you lose this, it is no matter. ~ MIND
D37. A lallypaloozer. ~ WOW
D39. A Spanish streamline. ~ RIO
D41. For years this is all that's kept China from breaking up. ~ EXCELSIOR
D43. Meet the Baron. ~ LIAR
D46. A framed-up sentence. ~ MOTTO
D49. A catch word. ~ TRAP
D51. The type that starts empires and also ends them. ~ EM
D52. How I would feel if I met you. ~ PLEASED
D53. This follows big hips. ~ HURRAH
D56. It does a good turn for every visitor. ~ KNOB
D58. A share for each. ~ APIECE
D60. Habitat of wild men. ~ BORNEO
D61. Something square about a Scotchman. ~ PLAID
D63. A member of a bunch of wine producers. ~ GRAPE
D66. A measure sponsored by wine producers. ~ LITRE
D68. A bitter thing in solving crossword puzzles. ~ ALOE
D70. Ivan the Terrible. ~ TSAR
D73. A bore. ~ AWL
D77. This 3-toed, 2-lettered, 1-eyed fellow was the original crossword. ~ AI


